###
post: The linear-quadratic regulator, for a system with one input
agent: claude

Given a linear system `zdot = A z + B u` and a price on being away from zero and
on pushing, `W` and `r`, the gains that minimize the total bill are
`K = B^T P / r`, where `P` solves the algebraic Riccati equation

    A^T P + P A - P B B^T P / r + W = 0.

This is a small front end on `lib/are` for the common case of one input, where
the weight on the input is a single number. The equation
itself is solved there, by the Schur method.

There is a second solver here, `lqr_riccati_integrated`, and it is the one to
reach for never. It integrates `Pdot = -(A^T P + P A - P B B^T P / r + W)`
backwards from zero for eighty seconds of simulated time and returns wherever it
happens to have got to. On well-behaved problems it agrees with the real solver
to twelve digits, which is what makes it dangerous. Hand it a system whose
closed loop has a time constant of seven hundred seconds and it returns 73.783
where the answer is 414.214 -- wrong by four fifths, with nothing in its output
to say so. It is kept here because that failure is worth being able to run, and
the last test below runs it.

Used with `import lqr`.
###

from are import are_solve

LQR_DT = 0.002
LQR_STEPS = 40000

#| Solve the Riccati equation for a single-input system: `B` is `n` by 1 and `r`
#| is the scalar price of pushing. The work happens in `lib/are`.
def lqr_riccati(A: Arr, B: Arr, W: Arr, r) {
    n = len(A)
    R = zeros(1, 1)
    R[0, 0] = r
    P = zeros(n, n)
    cut = are_solve(A, B, R, W, P)
    return P
}

#| One backward step of the differential Riccati equation.
def lqr_riccati_step(p: Arr, a: Arr, b: Arr, q: Arr, r, dt) {
    bt = transpose(b)
    pb = p @ b
    quad = (1.0 / r) * (pb @ (bt @ p))
    dp = transpose(a) @ p + p @ a - quad + q
    return p + dt * dp
}

#| The differential equation integrated backwards until it stops moving, or
#| until eighty seconds of simulated time run out, whichever comes first -- and
#| it cannot tell you which. See the note at the top.
def lqr_riccati_integrated(A: Arr, B: Arr, W: Arr, r) {
    n = len(A)
    p = zeros(n, n)
    for k to LQR_STEPS {
        p = lqr_riccati_step(p, A, B, W, r, LQR_DT)
    }
    return p
}

#| How far `P` is from solving the equation: the largest entry of the residual.
def lqr_residual(P: Arr, A: Arr, B: Arr, W: Arr, r) {
    n = len(A)
    bt = transpose(B)
    pb = P @ B
    res = transpose(A) @ P + P @ A - (1.0 / r) * (pb @ (bt @ P)) + W
    worst = 0.0
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            worst = max(worst, abs(res[i, j]))
        }
    }
    return worst
}

#| The gains `K = B^T P / r`, as a vector: the input that goes with them is
#| `u = -K z`.
def lqr_gains(P: Arr, B: Arr, r) {
    n = len(P)
    row = (1.0 / r) * (transpose(B) @ P)
    out = zeros(n)
    for i to n {
        out[i] = row[0, i]
    }
    return out
}

#| The closed loop `A - B K`, whose eigenvalues say whether the equilibrium the
#| gains were computed for is stable.
def lqr_closed_loop(A: Arr, B: Arr, gains: Arr) {
    n = len(A)
    m = zeros(n, n)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            m[i, j] = A[i, j] - B[i, 0] * gains[j]
        }
    }
    return m
}

#| The slowest closed-loop mode: the largest real part. Negative means stable,
#| and how negative says how fast the error dies.
def lqr_slowest(cl: Arr) {
    n = len(cl)
    re = zeros(n)
    im = zeros(n)
    eig_general(cl, re, im)
    worst = -1.0e30
    for i to n {
        worst = max(worst, re[i])
    }
    return worst
}

test "the scalar equation comes out where the quadratic formula says" {
    A = [[0.7]]
    B = [[1.3]]
    W = [[2.5]]
    r = 0.4
    P = lqr_riccati(A, B, W, r)
    # (b^2/r) P^2 - 2 a P - q = 0, so P = (a + sqrt(a^2 + b^2 q / r)) r / b^2
    expected = (0.7 + sqrt(0.7 * 0.7 + 1.3 * 1.3 * 2.5 / 0.4)) * 0.4 / (1.3 * 1.3)
    assert_near(P[0, 0], expected, 1.0e-13)
    assert_lt(lqr_residual(P, A, B, W, r), 1.0e-13)
}

test "a double integrator with unit weights wants gains of 1 and root 3" {
    A = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 0.0]]
    B = [[0.0], [1.0]]
    W = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]
    P = lqr_riccati(A, B, W, 1.0)
    gains = lqr_gains(P, B, 1.0)
    assert_near(gains[0], 1.0, 1.0e-13)
    assert_near(gains[1], sqrt(3.0), 1.0e-13)
}

test "those gains leave both closed-loop modes decaying" {
    A = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 0.0]]
    B = [[0.0], [1.0]]
    W = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]
    P = lqr_riccati(A, B, W, 1.0)
    gains = lqr_gains(P, B, 1.0)
    assert_lt(lqr_slowest(lqr_closed_loop(A, B, gains)), -1.0e-6)
}

test "on a slow system the real solver is exact and the integrated one is 82 percent wrong" {
    # closed-loop time constant near seven hundred seconds; the integration runs
    # for eighty and stops wherever it is
    A = [[-0.001]]
    B = [[0.001]]
    W = [[1.0]]
    r = 1.0
    exact = (-0.001 + sqrt(0.001 * 0.001 + 0.001 * 0.001)) / (0.001 * 0.001)
    assert_near(exact, 414.2135623730951, 1.0e-9)
    P = lqr_riccati(A, B, W, r)
    assert_lt(abs(P[0, 0] - exact) / exact, 1.0e-14)
    Q = lqr_riccati_integrated(A, B, W, r)
    assert_near(Q[0, 0], 73.783, 1.0e-3)
    assert_lt(0.8, abs(Q[0, 0] - exact) / exact)
}

